  "facts": [
    {
      "id": 1,
      "text": "Toughkenamon grew around the Philadelphia and Baltimore Railroad, with Isaac Slack buying land and building houses to secure a regular train stop.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        11,
        12,
        13
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.newgarden.org/241/Toughkenamon-Village",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The village name derives from the Lenape word for “firebrand,” first noted in an 18th‑century survey as “Dochranamon.”.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        19,
        21
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1",
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.newgarden.org/241/Toughkenamon-Village",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "In the 1850s Slack ran a brick wheel factory, a steam‑powered sawmill, and a 132‑acre farm that housed his workers.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6,
        7
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.newgarden.org/241/Toughkenamon-Village",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "By the early 1900s Toughkenamon’s economy pivoted to mushrooms, with a basket factory and Losito’s cannery supplying local jobs.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
